---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---
<Base
  title="NVLink & NVSwitch"
  description="Why 18 ports per GPU and 18 switch ASICs produce a single-layer non-blocking fabric across 72 GPUs, and what 130 TB/s is actually worth."
  kicker="Chapter 05"
  heading="NVLink & NVSwitch"
  slug="/nvlink"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      This is the chapter the rest of the rack exists to serve. The arithmetic is small enough to
      do in your head, and once you have done it the design stops looking like a specification and
      starts looking like the only sensible answer.
    </p>

    <h2 id="arithmetic">Eighteen and eighteen</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Every GPU has eighteen separate connections leaving it. The rack contains eighteen switch
        chips. Wire the first connection of every GPU to the first switch, the second to the
        second, and so on, and something neat happens: every GPU is one hop from every other GPU,
        and no two conversations have to share a wire.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is what people mean when they say the rack has no internal bottleneck. It is not a
        claim about being fast; it is a claim about there being nowhere for traffic to pile up.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Each Blackwell GPU exposes <S id="nvlink-links" /> NVLink 5 ports at 100 GB/s
        bidirectional, totalling <S id="nvlink-per-gpu" />. Each NVSwitch tray carries two
        NVSwitch5 ASICs, and with <S id="switch-trays" /> trays that is
        <S id="switch-asics" /> ASICs — exactly the port count per GPU.<Cite ids="nv-nvlink" />
      </p>
      <p>
        Connect port <em>n</em> of each GPU to switch <em>n</em>, and every switch sees all 72
        GPUs exactly once. Each ASIC has 36 + 36 ports and <S id="nvswitch-asic" /> of switching
        capacity. The fabric is one layer deep, fully connected, and
        <T id="non-blocking">non-blocking</T>: any permutation of senders and receivers runs at
        line rate. Aggregate in-rack bandwidth is <S id="alltoall" />.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Fabric explorer"
    hint="Hover a GPU · click to keep it"
    caption="Each of a GPU's 18 NVLink ports terminates on a different NVSwitch ASIC, so all 18 are usable at once toward any set of peers. Drawn as 18 trays of four GPUs against 9 switch trays of two ASICs; link lines are schematic, the copper spine carries over 5,000 of them."
  >
    <div data-fabric>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 760 420" role="img"
        aria-label="Diagram of 72 GPUs in 18 trays on the left connected to 18 NVSwitch ASICs in 9 trays on the right. Selecting a GPU draws one link to each of the 18 switch ASICs.">
        <text x="8" y="14">18 compute trays · 72 GPUs</text>
        <text x="752" y="14" text-anchor="end">9 NVSwitch trays · 18 ASICs</text>
        <g data-links stroke="var(--copper)" fill="none"></g>
        <g data-gpus></g>
        <g data-switches></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Selected</dt><dd data-out="sel">—</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Links from this GPU</dt><dd data-out="links">18</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Bandwidth, all ports</dt><dd data-out="bw">1.8 TB/s</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Reachable in one hop</dt><dd data-out="hop">71 GPUs</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Link display">
        <button type="button" data-mode="one" aria-pressed="true">One GPU</button>
        <button type="button" data-mode="all" aria-pressed="false">All 1,296 links</button>
      </div>
      <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">72 GPUs × 18 ports = 1,296 endpoint links, before the spine's cable count.</span>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Worth stating what this removes rather than what it adds. There is no rail assignment, no
        leaf/spine oversubscription ratio, no bisection figure to derate collectives against, and
        no distinction between "near" and "far" peers within the rack. A parallelism strategy can
        be chosen on numerical and load-balance grounds instead of being contorted to keep
        high-traffic dimensions inside an 8-GPU node.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one thing it does not remove is the rack boundary. Inside, per-GPU egress is 900 GB/s;
        across racks it is 50 GB/s on ConnectX-7 or 100 GB/s on ConnectX-8 — roughly a factor of
        nine to eighteen. Every serious deployment decision at this scale is about which model
        dimension gets to cross that line.
        <a href="/scale-out">Chapter 7</a> takes that up.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="sharp">The switch does arithmetic</h2>

    <p>
      An <S id="nvswitch-asic" bare /> Tb/s switch ASIC looks underspecified next to a 51.2 Tb/s
      Ethernet switch of the same era. The missing capacity went somewhere deliberate.
    </p>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        NVSwitch carries <T id="sharp">SHARP</T> engines, which perform reductions and multicast
        <em>inside</em> the switch. In an all-reduce — the collective that dominates distributed
        training — partial results are combined as they pass through the fabric instead of being
        shuttled between GPUs and summed at the endpoints. That roughly halves the bytes on the
        wire and removes a round trip from the critical path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Trading port bandwidth for in-network compute is the right trade when your dominant traffic
        pattern is a collective rather than point-to-point. It is also why the spine's quoted
        AllReduce figure, <S id="allreduce" />, is higher than the all-to-all figure of
        <S id="alltoall" />: they are measuring different operations, not disagreeing.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="calculator">What the bandwidth is worth</h2>

    <p>
      Bandwidth numbers are hard to feel. This converts them into the thing you actually wait for:
      the time to complete one all-to-all exchange, the pattern that a
      <T id="moe">mixture-of-experts</T> layer performs on every forward pass.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="All-to-all exchange time"
    hint="Drag the payload"
    caption="A first-order model: time ≈ bytes each GPU must send ÷ that GPU's egress bandwidth. NVLink's 1.8 TB/s is a bidirectional figure, so egress is 900 GB/s; the scale-out numbers are per-SuperNIC line rates. Real collectives add protocol and synchronisation overhead, so treat these as lower bounds and as ratios rather than as predictions."
  >
    <div data-bwcalc>
      <dl class="readout">
        <div><dt>Payload per GPU</dt><dd data-out="payload">256 MB</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Egress bandwidth</dt><dd data-out="bw">900 GB/s</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Exchange time</dt><dd data-out="time">0.28 ms</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Versus NVLink</dt><dd data-out="ratio">1.0×</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 150" style="margin-top:1rem" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart comparing exchange time across four interconnects.">
        <g data-bars></g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="payload">Payload per GPU<output data-out="payload-lbl">256 MB</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="payload" data-payload min="0" max="100" value="50" />
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Interconnect">
        <button type="button" data-fab="nvlink" aria-pressed="true">NVLink 5</button>
        <button type="button" data-fab="cx8" aria-pressed="false">ConnectX-8</button>
        <button type="button" data-fab="cx7" aria-pressed="false">ConnectX-7</button>
        <button type="button" data-fab="pcie" aria-pressed="false">PCIe Gen5 ×16</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"72 GPUs sharing one link."</strong> The opposite: every GPU holds
        <S id="nvlink-per-gpu" /> to the fabric simultaneously, and because the ports fan out across
        all 18 switches, no peer selection reduces that. The aggregate <S id="alltoall" /> is not a
        shared pipe being divided; it is 72 GPUs each able to move data at full rate at once.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="numbers">Interconnect figures</h2>

    <SpecTable
      caption="NVLink 5 and NVSwitch5"
      ids={['nvlink-per-gpu', 'nvlink-links', 'nvswitch-asic', 'switch-asics', 'alltoall', 'allreduce', 'spine-cables', 'spine-cartridges', 'optical-penalty']}
    />

    <Deeper title="Copper, and the 20 kW that decided it">
      <p>
        The spine could have been optical. It is not, and the reason is a power calculation:
        transceivers and retimers for the NVLink spine would have drawn roughly
        <S id="optical-penalty" />.<Cite ids="semianalysis-optical" /> SemiAnalysis reached
        ~19.4 kW independently, counting 648 1.6T transceivers at about 30 W each.
      </p>
      <p>
        Against <S id="rack-power" /> that is about a sixth of the rack's budget spent moving data
        a metre and a half. At that reach copper is both far cheaper and far more efficient — and
        the price of using it is that the switches must sit in the middle of the rack and the trays
        must be close-packed around them. The rack's physical arrangement is downstream of this
        one decision.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    /* ── Fabric explorer ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    const fab = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-fabric]');
    if (fab) {
      const figFab = fab.closest('figure');
      const svg = fab.querySelector('svg')!;
      const gGpu = fab.querySelector('[data-gpus]')!;
      const gSw = fab.querySelector('[data-switches]')!;
      const gLink = fab.querySelector('[data-links]')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };

      const TOP = 26, ROW = 21, GW = 15, GH = 13;
      const gpuPos: { x: number; y: number; tray: number; i: number }[] = [];
      const gpuRects: SVGRectElement[] = [];
      for (let t = 0; t < 18; t++) {
        const y = TOP + t * ROW;
        gGpu.appendChild(el('text', { x: 8, y: y + 10, 'font-size': 9, 'data-tray-label': t }))
          .textContent = String(t + 1).padStart(2, '0');
        for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
          const x = 30 + i * (GW + 4);
          const r = el('rect', { x, y, width: GW, height: GH, rx: 2, fill: 'var(--gpu)', opacity: 0.55,
            'data-gpu': t * 4 + i, tabindex: 0, role: 'button',
            'aria-label': `GPU ${t * 4 + i + 1}, tray ${t + 1}` });
          gGpu.appendChild(r);
          gpuRects.push(r as SVGRectElement);
          gpuPos.push({ x: x + GW, y: y + GH / 2, tray: t, i });
        }
      }
      const swPos: { x: number; y: number }[] = [];
      for (let s = 0; s < 18; s++) {
        const y = TOP + 4 + s * ROW;
        const x = 660;
        gSw.appendChild(el('rect', { x, y, width: 46, height: 13, rx: 2, fill: 'var(--switch)',
          opacity: 0.9, 'data-sw': s }));
        gSw.appendChild(el('text', { x: x + 52, y: y + 10, 'font-size': 9 })).textContent = 'sw' + String(s + 1).padStart(2, '0');
        swPos.push({ x, y: y + 6.5 });
      }

      const draw = (indices: number[], opacity: number) => {
        gLink.textContent = '';
        for (const g of indices) {
          const p = gpuPos[g];
          for (const s of swPos) {
            const mid = (p.x + s.x) / 2;
            gLink.appendChild(el('path', {
              d: `M${p.x} ${p.y} C ${mid} ${p.y}, ${mid} ${s.y}, ${s.x} ${s.y}`,
              'stroke-width': 0.7, opacity,
            }));
          }
        }
      };
      const out = (k: string) => fab.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;

      // Same mute level as the 3D rack and the tray diagram.
      const MUTED = '0.1';
      const trayLabels = [...gGpu.querySelectorAll<SVGTextElement>('[data-tray-label]')];
      const swRects = [...gSw.querySelectorAll<SVGRectElement>('[data-sw]')];

      /**
       * Which GPU a click has fixed in place, if any. Hovering still previews
       * any GPU; leaving the drawing comes back to this one.
       */
      let pinned: number | null = null;

      // Drawn as its own element on top rather than as a stroke on the GPU
      // rect: a pinned GPU is muted while you preview another, and opacity on
      // the rect would take its ring down with it.
      const pin = el('rect', {
        fill: 'none', stroke: 'var(--copper)', 'stroke-width': 1.5, rx: 4, visibility: 'hidden',
      });
      gGpu.appendChild(pin);
      const placePin = () => {
        if (pinned === null) { pin.setAttribute('visibility', 'hidden'); return; }
        const r = gpuRects[pinned];
        pin.setAttribute('x', String(Number(r.getAttribute('x')) - 3));
        pin.setAttribute('y', String(Number(r.getAttribute('y')) - 3));
        pin.setAttribute('width', String(GW + 6));
        pin.setAttribute('height', String(GH + 6));
        pin.setAttribute('visibility', 'visible');
      };

      const select = (g: number | null) => {
        // The selected GPU is left alone and its 71 peers mute back. The
        // switch column stays lit on purpose: the whole claim of this figure
        // is that one GPU reaches all eighteen of them, so they are the far
        // end of the selection rather than something competing with it.
        gGpu.querySelectorAll<SVGRectElement>('[data-gpu]').forEach((r) => {
          r.setAttribute('opacity', g !== null && Number(r.dataset.gpu) === g ? '1' : MUTED);
        });
        for (const t of trayLabels) {
          const own = g !== null && Number(t.dataset.trayLabel) === gpuPos[g].tray;
          t.setAttribute('opacity', g === null || own ? '1' : MUTED);
        }
        for (const r of swRects) r.setAttribute('opacity', g === null ? '0.9' : '1');
        placePin();
        if (g === null) { draw([], 0); out('sel').textContent = '—'; return; }
        draw([g], 0.75);
        out('sel').textContent =
          `GPU ${g + 1} · tray ${gpuPos[g].tray + 1}${g === pinned ? ' · pinned' : ''}`;
      };

      /** "All links" has no single subject, so nothing is muted. */
      const showAll = () => {
        pinned = null;
        placePin();
        gGpu.querySelectorAll<SVGRectElement>('[data-gpu]').forEach((r) => r.setAttribute('opacity', '0.55'));
        for (const t of trayLabels) t.setAttribute('opacity', '1');
        for (const r of swRects) r.setAttribute('opacity', '0.9');
        draw([...Array(72).keys()], 0.045);
        out('sel').textContent = 'all 72';
      };
      const togglePin = (g: number) => {
        pinned = pinned === g ? null : g;
        select(pinned ?? g);
      };
      gpuRects.forEach((r, g) => {
        r.style.cursor = 'pointer';
        r.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => select(g));
        r.addEventListener('focus', () => select(g));
        r.addEventListener('click', () => togglePin(g));
        r.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
          if (e.key !== 'Enter' && e.key !== ' ') return;
          e.preventDefault();
          togglePin(g);
        });
      });

      let mode = 'one';
      figFab?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-mode]').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          mode = b.dataset.mode!;
          figFab!.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-mode]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          if (mode === 'all') {
            showAll();
          } else {
            select(pinned ?? 0);
          }
        });
      });
      svg.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => { if (mode === 'one') select(pinned ?? 0); });
      select(0);
    }

    /* ── All-to-all calculator ───────────────────────────────────────── */
    const calc = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-bwcalc]');
    if (calc) {
      // Per-GPU egress bandwidth in bytes/s. NVLink's headline 1.8 TB/s is
      // bidirectional, so one direction is 900 GB/s.
      const FAB: Record<string, { label: string; bw: number }> = {
        nvlink: { label: 'NVLink 5, in-rack', bw: 900e9 },
        cx8: { label: 'ConnectX-8, 800 Gb/s', bw: 100e9 },
        cx7: { label: 'ConnectX-7, 400 Gb/s', bw: 50e9 },
        pcie: { label: 'PCIe Gen5 ×16', bw: 64e9 },
      };
      const figCalc = calc.closest('figure');
      // The slider lives in the controls row, a sibling of .widget-body.
      const slider = figCalc!.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-payload]')!;
      const bars = calc.querySelector('[data-bars]')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const out = (k: string) => calc.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const lbl = figCalc?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-out="payload-lbl"]');
      let current = 'nvlink';

      // 1 MB … 8 GB, logarithmic.
      const payloadBytes = () => 1e6 * Math.pow(8000, Number(slider.value) / 100);
      const fmtBytes = (b: number) => b >= 1e9 ? (b / 1e9).toFixed(2) + ' GB' : (b / 1e6).toFixed(0) + ' MB';
      const fmtTime = (s: number) => s >= 1 ? s.toFixed(2) + ' s' : s >= 1e-3 ? (s * 1e3).toFixed(2) + ' ms' : (s * 1e6).toFixed(0) + ' µs';

      const render = () => {
        const bytes = payloadBytes();
        const base = bytes / FAB.nvlink.bw;
        const f = FAB[current];
        const t = bytes / f.bw;
        out('payload').textContent = fmtBytes(bytes);
        if (lbl) lbl.textContent = fmtBytes(bytes);
        out('bw').textContent = (f.bw / 1e9).toFixed(0) + ' GB/s';
        out('time').textContent = fmtTime(t);
        out('ratio').textContent = (t / base).toFixed(1) + '× slower';

        bars.textContent = '';
        const keys = Object.keys(FAB);
        const worst = Math.max(...keys.map((k) => bytes / FAB[k].bw));
        keys.forEach((k, i) => {
          const y = i * 34 + 8;
          const time = bytes / FAB[k].bw;
          const w = Math.max(2, (time / worst) * 470);
          const rect = document.createElementNS(NS, 'rect');
          rect.setAttribute('x', '180'); rect.setAttribute('y', String(y));
          rect.setAttribute('width', String(w)); rect.setAttribute('height', '18');
          rect.setAttribute('rx', '3');
          rect.setAttribute('fill', k === current ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--line-strong)');
          bars.appendChild(rect);
          const name = document.createElementNS(NS, 'text');
          name.setAttribute('x', '172'); name.setAttribute('y', String(y + 13));
          name.setAttribute('text-anchor', 'end');
          name.textContent = FAB[k].label;
          bars.appendChild(name);
          const val = document.createElementNS(NS, 'text');
          val.setAttribute('x', String(186 + w)); val.setAttribute('y', String(y + 13));
          val.textContent = fmtTime(time);
          bars.appendChild(val);
        });
      };
      slider.addEventListener('input', render);
      figCalc?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-fab]').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          current = b.dataset.fab!;
          figCalc!.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-fab]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          render();
        });
      });
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
